fix: TRBL-104 ledger status/verify now truly read-only

SPEC-01 §2.4 mandates that `status` and `verify` are read-only operations
that must not take LOCK_EX, write lifecycle records, rewrite .HEAD/.idx,
or report the caller as writer_pid. Previously these CLI verbs called
`openLedgerForReading()` which passed a `Writer` to `ledger.Open()`, causing
it to take an exclusive lock, write boot lifecycle records, and report itself
as the writer.

Changes:
- Add `ReadStatus(root)` — walks day files without locking, reads LOCK file
  (no flock) for the actual writer PID, returns LedgerStatus with correct
  writer identity
- Add `ReadVerify(root, day)` — walks day files without locking, checks seq
  monotonicity, validates torn/corrupt lines, verifies payload hashes
- Add `CheckWriterHeld(root)` — reads LOCK file (no flock) to check if a
  writer exists; used to produce "unreadable" row when daemon is live
- Refactor `cmdLedgerStatus` and `cmdLedgerVerify` to use read-only paths
- `cmdLedgerCompact` remains unchanged (it IS a write operation)

Before: `troubled ledger status --json` on a live daemon would take LOCK_EX,
append lifecycle{op:boot}, and report writer_pid=the CLI process PID.
After: status reads LOCK file without flock, reports the real writer PID,
writes nothing to the ledger.

Addresses TRBL-104.
